  "body": "Please specify what `tel:` URLs need to be blocked from being passed to a helper app, such as the system dialer.\r\n\r\nThere are special codes that the user can enter via the dialer to cause the phone, the SIM or their operator to take action other than placing a call. These action can be destructive (reset phone) or privacy-sensitive (redirect calls). It's bad if Web sites can trick users into invoking such actions via `tel:` URLs.\r\n\r\nAs far as I know, these codes start with a `*` or `#`. However, these characters can legitimately appear later in `tel:` URLs whose purpose is to place a call and then DTMF into the channel e.g. to join a conference call. (What about phone numbers that accept DTMF to perform more destructive or costly actions, though?)\r\n\r\nI suggest the spec say that `tel:` URLs must be blocked unless they start with (after parsing to normalize the scheme to lower case) `tel:+` followed by an ASCII digit or `tel:` followed by an ASCII digit.\r\n\r\n[Gecko bug](https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=797034), [Chromium bug](https://bugs.chromium.org/p/chromium/issues/detail?id=746427).",
